1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:01,720
harm.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:06,560
And this was sung every Sunday morning after the hoisting of the flag.

3
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:11,399
But somehow, neither the words nor the tune ever seemed to the animals to come

4
00:00:11,399 --> 00:00:15,239
up to Beasts of England.

5
00:00:15,239 --> 00:00:18,159
Chapter 8

6
00:00:19,239 --> 00:00:24,159
A few days later, when the terror caused by the executions had died down,

7
00:00:24,159 --> 00:00:28,640
some of the animals remembered, or thought they remembered, that the Sixth

8
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:34,720
Commandment decreed No animal shall kill any other animal.

9
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:38,880
And though no one cared to mention it in the hearing of the pigs or the dogs,

10
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:44,079
it was felt that the killings which had taken place did not square with this.

11
00:00:44,079 --> 00:00:49,039
Clover asked Benjamin to read her the Sixth Commandment, and when Benjamin, as

12
00:00:49,039 --> 00:00:54,960
usual, said that he refused to meddle in such matters, she fetched Muriel.

13
00:00:54,960 --> 00:01:04,359
Muriel read the Commandment for her. It ran No animal shall kill any other animal without cause.

14
00:01:04,359 --> 00:01:10,560
Somehow or other, the last two words had slipped out of the animals' memory.

15
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:15,199
But they saw now that the commandment had not been violated.

16
00:01:15,199 --> 00:01:19,399
For clearly there was good reason for killing the traitors who had leagued

17
00:01:19,399 --> 00:01:22,159
themselves with Snowball.

18
00:01:22,159 --> 00:01:27,960
Throughout the year, the animals worked even harder than they had worked in the previous year.

19
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:33,359
To rebuild the windmill with walls twice as thick as before, and to finish it by

20
00:01:33,359 --> 00:01:40,079
the appointed date, together with the regular work of the farm, was a tremendous labor.

21
00:01:40,079 --> 00:01:44,359
There were times when it seemed to the animals that they worked longer hours

22
00:01:44,359 --> 00:01:48,479
and fared no better than they had done in Jones's day.

23
00:01:48,479 --> 00:01:53,680
On Sunday mornings, Squealer, holding down a long strip of paper with his trotter,

24
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:57,960
would read out to them lists of figures proving that the production of every

25
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:07,600
class of foodstuff had increased by 200%, 300%, or 500%, as the case might be.

26
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:12,359
The animals saw no reason to disbelieve him, especially as they could no longer

27
00:02:12,359 --> 00:02:17,720
remember very clearly what conditions had been like before the rebellion.

28
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:21,399
All the same, there were days when they felt that they would sooner have had

29
00:02:21,399 --> 00:02:24,319
less figures and more food.

30
00:02:24,319 --> 00:02:28,960
All orders were now issued through Squealer or one of the other pigs.

31
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:33,880
Napoleon himself was not seen in public as often as once in a fortnight.

32
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:38,680
When he did appear, he was attended not only by his retinue of dogs, but by a

33
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:44,319
black cockerel who marched in front of him and acted as a kind of trumpeter,

34
00:02:44,319 --> 00:02:48,359
letting out a loud "Cock-a-doodle-doo!"

35
00:02:48,359 --> 00:02:51,119
before Napoleon spoke.

36
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:57,359
Even in the farmhouse, it was said, Napoleon inhabited separate apartments from the others.

37
00:02:57,359 --> 00:03:01,479
He took his meals alone, with two dogs to wait upon him,

38
00:03:01,479 --> 00:03:05,119
and always ate from the Crown Derby dinner service, which had been in the

39
00:03:05,119 --> 00:03:08,079
glass cupboard in the drawing room.

40
00:03:08,079 --> 00:03:13,359
It was also announced that the gun would be fired every year on Napoleon's birthday

41
00:03:13,359 --> 00:03:16,039
as well as on the other two anniversaries.

42
00:03:16,039 --> 00:03:20,560
Napoleon was now never spoken of simply as Napoleon.

43
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:27,359
He was always referred to in formal style as our leader, Comrade Napoleon.

44
00:03:27,359 --> 00:03:36,640
And the pigs liked to invent for him such titles as father of all animals, terror of mankind,

45
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:42,720
protector of the sheepfold, ducklings' friend, and the like.

46
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:47,800
In his speeches, Squealer would talk with the tears rolling down his cheeks

47
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:54,760
of Napoleon's wisdom, the goodness of his heart, and the deep love he bore to all animals

48
00:03:54,760 --> 00:04:02,720
everywhere, even and especially the unhappy animals who still lived in ignorance and slavery

49
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:05,159
on other farms.

50
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:10,039
It had become usual to give Napoleon the credit for every successful achievement

51
00:04:10,039 --> 00:04:13,239
and every stroke of good fortune.

52
00:04:13,239 --> 00:04:18,640
You would often hear one hen remark to another, >> "Under the guidance of our leader,

53
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:24,199
Comrade Napoleon, I have laid five eggs in six days."

54
00:04:24,199 --> 00:04:28,279
>> Or two cows enjoying a drink at the pool would exclaim,

55
00:04:28,279 --> 00:04:34,720
>> "Thanks to the leadership of Comrade Napoleon, how excellent this water tastes."

56
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:39,800
>> The general feeling on the farm was well expressed in a poem entitled Comrade

57
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:45,439
Napoleon, which was composed by Minimus and which ran as follows.

58
00:04:45,439 --> 00:04:50,840
>> Friend of the fatherless, fountain of happiness, lord of the swill bucket. Oh,

59
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:55,520
how my soul is on fire when I gaze at thy calm and commanding eye. Like the

60
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:01,800
sun in the sky, Comrade Napoleon, thou art the giver of all that thy creatures love.

61
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:07,960
Full belly twice a day, clean straw to roll upon, every beast, great or small, sleeps at

62
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:09,720
peace in his stall.

63
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:14,239
Thou watchest over all, Comrade Napoleon.

64
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:18,239
Had I a sucking pig ere he had grown as

65
00:05:18,239 --> 00:05:22,680
big even as a pint bottle or as a rolling pin, he should have learned to

66
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:29,000
be faithful and true to thee. Yes, his first squeak should be Comrade Napoleon.

67
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,558
>> Napoleon approved of this poem and caused it to be inscribed on the wall of

68
00:05:33,558 --> 00:05:38,440
the big barn at the opposite end from the seven commandments.

69
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:47,558
It was surmounted by a portrait of Napoleon in profile, executed by Squealer in white paint.

70
00:05:49,239 --> 00:05:53,399
Meanwhile, through the agency of Whymper, Napoleon was engaged in

71
00:05:53,399 --> 00:05:57,360
complicated negotiations with Frederick and Pilkington.

72
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:00,319
The pile of timber was still unsold.

73
00:06:00,319 --> 00:06:04,200
Of the two, Frederick was the more anxious to get hold of it,

74
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,239
but he would not offer a reasonable price.

75
00:06:07,239 --> 00:06:11,319
At the same time, there were renewed rumors that Frederick and his men were

76
00:06:11,319 --> 00:06:16,799
plotting to attack Animal Farm and to destroy the windmill, the building of which had aroused

77
00:06:16,799 --> 00:06:19,079
furious jealousy in him.

78
00:06:19,079 --> 00:06:23,159
Snowball was known to be still skulking on Pinchfield Farm.

79
00:06:23,159 --> 00:06:27,000
In the middle of the summer, the animals were alarmed to hear that three hens had

80
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,638
come forward and confessed that, inspired by Snowball, they had entered

81
00:06:31,638 --> 00:06:34,479
into a plot to murder Napoleon.

82
00:06:34,479 --> 00:06:40,239
They were executed immediately, and fresh precautions for Napoleon's safety were taken.

83
00:06:40,239 --> 00:06:44,159
Four dogs guarded his bed at night, one at each corner,

84
00:06:44,159 --> 00:06:48,599
and a young pig named Pinkeye was given the task of tasting all his food before

85
00:06:48,599 --> 00:06:51,799
he ate it, lest it should be poisoned.

86
00:06:51,799 --> 00:06:55,558
At about the same time, it was given out that Napoleon had arranged to sell the

87
00:06:55,558 --> 00:06:58,040
pile of timber to Mr. Pilkington.

88
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:01,840
He was also going to enter into a regular agreement for the exchange of

89
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:06,558
certain products between Animal Farm and Foxwood.

90
00:07:06,558 --> 00:07:10,399
The relations between Napoleon and Pilkington, though they were only

91
00:07:10,399 --> 00:07:14,679
conducted through Whymper, were now almost friendly.

92
00:07:14,679 --> 00:07:19,280
The animals distrusted Pilkington as a human being, but greatly preferred him

93
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:23,319
to Frederick, whom they both feared and hated.

94
00:07:23,319 --> 00:07:27,799
As the summer wore on and the windmill neared completion, the rumors of an

95
00:07:27,799 --> 00:07:32,440
impending treacherous attack grew stronger and stronger.

96
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:37,519
Frederick, it was said, intended to bring against them 20 men all armed with

97
00:07:37,519 --> 00:07:42,319
guns, and he had already bribed the magistrates and police so that if he

98
00:07:42,319 --> 00:07:48,239
could once get hold of the title deeds of Animal Farm, they would ask no questions.

99
00:07:48,239 --> 00:07:53,638
Moreover, terrible stories were leaking out from Pinchfield about the cruelties that

100
00:07:53,638 --> 00:07:56,718
Frederick practiced upon his animals.

101
00:07:56,718 --> 00:08:02,079
He had flogged an old horse to death. He starved his cows. He had killed a dog by

102
00:08:02,079 --> 00:08:04,239
throwing it into the furnace.

103
00:08:04,239 --> 00:08:08,519
He amused himself in the evenings by making fight with splinters of

104
00:08:08,519 --> 00:08:11,760
razor blade tied to their spurs.

105
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:16,519
The animals' blood boiled with rage when they heard of these things being done to

106
00:08:16,519 --> 00:08:22,239
their comrades, and sometimes they clamored to be allowed to go out in a body and attack

107
00:08:22,239 --> 00:08:28,000
Pinchfield Farm, drive out the humans, and set the animals free.

108
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,558
But Squealer counseled them to avoid rash actions and trust in Comrade Napoleon's strategy.

109
00:08:34,558 --> 00:08:39,479
Nevertheless, feeling against Frederick continued to run high.

110
00:08:39,479 --> 00:08:43,920
One Sunday morning, Napoleon appeared in the barn and explained that he had never

111
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:48,200
at any time contemplated selling the pile of timber to Frederick.

112
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:52,920
He considered it beneath his dignity, he said, to have dealings with scoundrels

113
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:55,079
of that description.

114
00:08:55,079 --> 00:08:58,760
The pigeons, who were still sent out to spread tidings of the rebellion, were

115
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:03,120
forbidden to set foot anywhere on Foxwood, and were also ordered to drop

116
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:10,719
their former slogan of death to humanity in favor of death to Frederick.

117
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:16,760
In the late summer, yet another of Snowball's machinations was laid bare.

118
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:21,280
The wheat crop was full of weeds, and it was discovered that on one of his

119
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:27,159
nocturnal visits, Snowball had mixed weed seeds with the seed corn.

120
00:09:27,159 --> 00:09:31,120
A gander, who had been privy to the plot, had confessed his guilt to

121
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:37,280
Squealer and immediately committed suicide by swallowing deadly nightshade berries.

122
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:41,879
The animals now also learned that Snowball had never, as many of them had

123
00:09:41,879 --> 00:09:49,479
believed hitherto, received the order of Animal Hero First Class.

124
00:09:50,079 --> 00:09:53,680
This was merely a legend which had been spread sometime after the Battle of the

125
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:56,639
Cowshed by Snowball himself.

126
00:09:56,639 --> 00:09:57,000
So far from being decorated, he had been
